[00:31:53] ## What was already on disk, and what I did with it
[00:31:53] The domain/application/web/frontend implementation and two new Java suites existed. I verified them against each acceptance criterion, found and fixed two things that did not actually work, added the missing coverage, measured the cost, and wrote the records.
[00:31:53] **Two defects in the existing work:**
[00:31:53] 1. `CraftingProfitServiceTest`'s recording planner still overrode the eight-argument `evaluateAllCoordinated` that the service no longer calls — it captured nothing and five tests failed with NPEs.
[00:31:53] 2. The new frontend test used a `data-test="resolution-node"` hook that does not exist (it is `tree-node`) and matched node text a parent also contains, so it would have asserted the *root's* reason with the child unmarked.
[00:31:53] **Coverage I added:** `TpTradeableItemRepositoryTest` (5 tests, real PostgreSQL — proves the classification follows `tp_tradeable_items` and not price data), two detail-association tests for the option-sensitive stale answer, four new steps in the profit browser check, and a second measured scenario in the real-DB perf IT.
[00:31:53] ## Implementation shape
[00:31:53] `craft.CraftingSettings.allowNonTradeableMaterials` (policy, default enabled) plus new `craft.MaterialTradeability` (classification from `repo.tp.TpTradeableItemRepository`), meeting only in `PlannerContext.excludesNonTradeableMaterial(itemId)`. Off, the resolver blocks a forbidden ingredient before any owned quantity is taken and before a path for it is attempted, and §30's candidate comparison runs a first pass over candidates consuming none — so a dearer tradeable-only alternative wins instead of losing on cost. The six-arg settings constructor, eight-arg planner/explainer overloads and adapter-free service constructor all remain, so JavaFX and Discovery are unchanged and issue no extra query.
